WEBVTT

00:00:05.108 --> 00:00:07.706
<v Joe Simpson Jr.> Greetings again from Southern California.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.892
My name is Joe Simpson Jr.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.854
I'm a volunteer wrangler for
WordPress Accessibility Day,

00:00:14.722 --> 00:00:19.899
I'm also a WordCamp WordPress Meetup
and WordCamp Community organizer

00:00:20.334 --> 00:00:21.920
as well as an accessibility advocate.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.763
Similar to Alicia's story
that we just heard in the last hour,

00:00:27.773 --> 00:00:32.910
Sumner Davenport shaped my
re-entry into accessibility as well.

00:00:34.353 --> 00:00:39.020
Now I know two people
in my entire life that are me.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.717
I have a workout friend
who is always ready to go,

00:00:45.742 --> 00:00:48.807
no matter rain or shine just like me,

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:51.603
no excuses.

00:00:52.547 --> 00:00:53.952
Sumner is that other person.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.786
She matches my desire to learn

00:00:59.811 --> 00:01:02.655
with inspiration, knowledge
and nurturing leadership

00:01:03.588 --> 00:01:05.280
here in the North Valleys of Los Angeles.

00:01:07.249 --> 00:01:11.890
Now I met Sumner,
I went to a meetup in West Hills

00:01:11.916 --> 00:01:13.310
to see her speak,

00:01:13.335 --> 00:01:17.267
because I was hosting our first
WordCamp in Santa Clarita

00:01:17.292 --> 00:01:18.700
and I had heard about her

00:01:18.724 --> 00:01:20.406
and the topic was very interesting.

00:01:22.803 --> 00:01:26.603
What happened at that meetup
is why I'm here before you today.

00:01:26.628 --> 00:01:29.386
She's that type of magnetic personality.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.354
She killed her presentation
and she said yes

00:01:37.066 --> 00:01:38.483
and presented at our meetup

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.451
and we've been close ever since.

00:01:41.192 --> 00:01:44.932
Sumner will present
The Profitability of Accessibility,

00:01:44.957 --> 00:01:47.223
but first let me tell you more
about this amazing woman.

00:01:48.404 --> 00:01:52.148
Sumner Davenport specializes
in web accessibility on WordPress.

00:01:52.742 --> 00:01:55.129
Her passion is to educate business owners

00:01:55.154 --> 00:01:57.302
and web designers using WordPress,

00:01:57.828 --> 00:02:02.148
on the increased value and
ease of an accessible site.

00:02:03.618 --> 00:02:05.780
Through her long-standing organization

00:02:05.805 --> 00:02:09.058
she and her team have built, maintained,

00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:12.434
evaluated and remediated,
dozens of WordPress websites

00:02:12.459 --> 00:02:15.501
to WCAG 2.1 success criterion.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.540
Her clients are referred by other clients
and from attorneys.

00:02:21.568 --> 00:02:22.768
She's an active…

00:02:23.428 --> 00:02:28.038
She's active in accessibility and legal
conferences and educational venues.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.340
At WordCamps and local WordPress groups,

00:02:33.364 --> 00:02:38.099
she regularly speaks on topics related
to compliance issues for WordPress.

00:02:39.413 --> 00:02:42.605
After presenting at WordCamp
Santa Clarita in 2019,

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.118
[Joe silently mouthed "my meetup"]

00:02:44.156 --> 00:02:47.531
she volunteered to be a speaker
wrangler at WordCamp Santa Clarita

00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:52.914
and is on the speaker wrangler
team for WordCamp Los Angeles 2020.

00:02:54.026 --> 00:02:57.366
She and two colleagues
founded a local meetup

00:02:57.391 --> 00:02:59.834
to discuss WordPress and accessibility

00:02:59.859 --> 00:03:01.658
and invited renowned speakers

00:03:01.683 --> 00:03:04.205
on accessibility topics
to be guest presenters.

00:03:05.146 --> 00:03:07.895
Partnering with two other
local meetup organizers

00:03:07.895 --> 00:03:10.538
they organized the first
annual mega meetup

00:03:10.563 --> 00:03:12.822
WordPress Accessibility Day 2020.

00:03:13.533 --> 00:03:18.310
She is a member of the International
Association of Accessibility Professionals,

00:03:18.335 --> 00:03:20.012
the IAAP,

00:03:20.037 --> 00:03:24.495
and is an active supporter of
various disability organizations.

00:03:25.824 --> 00:03:30.720
Please welcome Sumner Davenport presenting
The Profitability of Accessibility.

00:03:30.744 --> 00:03:31.965
Sumner take it away.

00:03:33.381 --> 00:03:38.085
<v Sumner Davenport> I don't know
if I can talk after that introduction Joe.

00:03:38.111 --> 00:03:40.425
Goodness, I'm glad I have my Kleenex handy.

00:03:40.449 --> 00:03:41.863
You really touched me

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:44.777
with the beginning of that introduction.

00:03:44.802 --> 00:03:46.406
Thank you so very very much.

00:03:46.852 --> 00:03:53.338
I am very honored to be here tonight.
I am so glad that I was selected to speak.

00:03:53.363 --> 00:03:56.734
Thank you so much to Joe Dolson
for putting this together,

00:03:56.759 --> 00:03:59.683
and for saying yes to me

00:03:59.708 --> 00:04:04.243
and of course, for Joe Simpson.
He is an inspiration to me a lot.

00:04:04.268 --> 00:04:05.996
So I'm going to jump into my topic.

00:04:06.021 --> 00:04:09.210
Let's see if I can get my
screen share to work here today.

00:04:09.235 --> 00:04:11.714
If there's any problem hearing me,

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:13.771
if you have any problem with my screen,

00:04:13.795 --> 00:04:19.674
make sure that you make a comment so
that one of the Joes here can let me know.

00:04:19.699 --> 00:04:21.893
If you have any questions as I go along,

00:04:21.918 --> 00:04:25.573
please put them in the chat
on the stream on YouTube,

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:27.708
so that Joe can let me know what those are,

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:31.801
because I want to be as
helpful as I possibly can

00:04:31.826 --> 00:04:33.570
during the time that we have tonight.

00:04:33.595 --> 00:04:36.236
So let's see if we can find my PowerPoint.

00:04:37.029 --> 00:04:40.080
So the topic that I was
going to talk about tonight

00:04:40.104 --> 00:04:42.767
is called
The Profitability of Accessibility

00:04:42.791 --> 00:04:45.140
and I feel very honored that last night,

00:04:45.165 --> 00:04:51.004
Joe Simpson took a few minutes to let
me do a kind of a test run on this topic.

00:04:51.029 --> 00:04:52.918
And I made a little bit of an edit,

00:04:52.942 --> 00:04:56.850
and then today another associate said,
hey let me hear what you're going to do

00:04:56.875 --> 00:04:58.792
because I can't be there tonight.

00:04:59.402 --> 00:05:00.434
So I did,

00:05:01.078 --> 00:05:03.141
and after hearing what I had to say,

00:05:03.166 --> 00:05:08.746
she kind of hemmed and hawed and
said hmm, I'm not thrilled with the title.

00:05:09.916 --> 00:05:13.580
I thought well you know,
but the title is Profitability.

00:05:14.666 --> 00:05:17.046
The profitability is the
degree to which a business

00:05:17.071 --> 00:05:19.706
or activity yields a
profit or a financial gain.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.733
Although accessibility—

00:05:23.758 --> 00:05:28.735
bringing accessibility to all
persons is really the right thing to do,

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:32.393
it's important if this is what
you're doing professionally,

00:05:32.418 --> 00:05:34.828
that you can make some money,

00:05:34.853 --> 00:05:36.359
you can have an income,

00:05:36.384 --> 00:05:38.659
you can thrive, so you can enjoy it.

00:05:39.248 --> 00:05:41.077
So yeah, profitability.

00:05:41.429 --> 00:05:45.377
So… I know, I thought
about it for a minute

00:05:45.401 --> 00:05:49.597
and then she said, I
think it's all in the title.

00:05:49.622 --> 00:05:51.597
I just don't like the title.

00:05:51.621 --> 00:05:54.875
So based on her input, I changed the title.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.107
So tonight, I didn't—
I did not warn Joe Dolson.

00:05:58.132 --> 00:05:59.411
I already sent in my slides.

00:05:59.435 --> 00:06:03.140
So I apologize if you are
following me on the slides online,

00:06:03.164 --> 00:06:05.161
this next slide is not part of it.

00:06:05.186 --> 00:06:06.187
Just one.

00:06:06.211 --> 00:06:08.406
it's now called, Show Me the Money,

00:06:09.188 --> 00:06:11.839
because isn't that what
people want to learn?

00:06:11.864 --> 00:06:14.975
If you as a web designer,
as a business owner,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.269
as an agency owner,

00:06:16.293 --> 00:06:21.295
if you start to put your efforts
into including accessibility

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:23.878
and do all the projects
you do for your client,

00:06:23.902 --> 00:06:25.586
don't you want to know

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:28.931
that there's a way that you
can get a return on investment

00:06:28.955 --> 00:06:30.837
on your time and your efforts?

00:06:31.356 --> 00:06:34.217
Well, I kind of figured
that was a better title

00:06:34.242 --> 00:06:38.206
and for anybody that knows me, it
kind of sounds more like me as well.

00:06:38.231 --> 00:06:41.015
So, tonight, we're going to talk about

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:44.224
how you can get a return
on investment on your time

00:06:44.249 --> 00:06:46.807
and your efforts with accessibility

00:06:46.832 --> 00:06:49.313
and how you can then also

00:06:49.756 --> 00:06:54.514
have a good way to explain
accessibility to your clients,

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:58.444
so that they also can see
the return on investment

00:06:58.469 --> 00:07:02.435
for bringing accessibility
into all of their digital products

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.787
and their company.

00:07:04.899 --> 00:07:08.944
So first off, just of course, we all know
this, but we're just going to touch on it,

00:07:08.969 --> 00:07:10.753
but what is accessibility?

00:07:10.777 --> 00:07:15.440
Accessibility is removing the barriers
that prevent people from having access,

00:07:15.466 --> 00:07:16.468
equal access.

00:07:16.492 --> 00:07:18.594
whether it's a building, an area,

00:07:18.619 --> 00:07:20.679
a service that's being provided,

00:07:20.708 --> 00:07:24.365
a website, an app, any digital product.

00:07:24.822 --> 00:07:29.249
And accessibility is taking that
initiative to create products

00:07:29.274 --> 00:07:33.267
that are usable by,
with people, by people,

00:07:34.067 --> 00:07:36.704
with the widest
possible range of abilities.

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:40.976
What some people may not know,
is when you think accessibility first,

00:07:41.848 --> 00:07:45.449
and think about making things
accessible to all people,

00:07:45.474 --> 00:07:48.675
you open so many avenues,

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:53.996
so many possibilities,
so much creativity and innovation,

00:07:55.089 --> 00:07:57.986
that accessibility creates good growth

00:07:59.222 --> 00:08:00.303
in a lot of ways.

00:08:00.327 --> 00:08:02.304
And I'll touch on some of those tonight.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.476
What is web accessibility specifically?

00:08:07.059 --> 00:08:12.118
We have seen wA11y,
sometimes the W is not there,

00:08:12.143 --> 00:08:14.083
it's just A11Y

00:08:14.107 --> 00:08:19.110
and what that means is that
between the A and the y

00:08:19.135 --> 00:08:21.531
there is actually 11 letters
that should be in there,

00:08:21.556 --> 00:08:25.018
because it spells out the
entire word, accessibility.

00:08:25.043 --> 00:08:26.967
We put the W in there for web.

00:08:27.526 --> 00:08:29.911
So when you see wA11y or A11y,

00:08:29.935 --> 00:08:32.052
whichever way you want to pronounce this,

00:08:32.077 --> 00:08:33.314
it's accessibility.

00:08:33.859 --> 00:08:34.899
It's about people.

00:08:35.713 --> 00:08:37.407
It's about making a difference.

00:08:38.345 --> 00:08:43.264
So web accessibility refers to the
website that is designed for everyone!

00:08:44.033 --> 00:08:48.303
We want everyone,
regardless of their ability or disability,

00:08:48.328 --> 00:08:51.062
to have the same ease of access.

00:08:51.786 --> 00:08:53.919
That's the most important part.

00:08:53.943 --> 00:08:57.676
It's the user experience
that everybody gets to have.

00:08:59.222 --> 00:09:03.569
So we also have to keep in mind
that web accessibility,

00:09:03.594 --> 00:09:07.148
it actually involves
a wide range of disabilities.

00:09:07.173 --> 00:09:10.742
So many times we hear people
talk about only screen readers,

00:09:10.767 --> 00:09:12.822
or blind individuals,

00:09:12.847 --> 00:09:15.005
or somebody needing captions,

00:09:15.029 --> 00:09:20.315
without taking into an understanding
that there are far more…

00:09:21.972 --> 00:09:25.622
areas and ranges of disabilities
that we need to consider.

00:09:28.319 --> 00:09:30.918
So then I get people that
say this to me all the time,

00:09:30.943 --> 00:09:33.440
rather sarcastically,
they unfortunately do.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:36.166
Is there really a need
for web accessibility?

00:09:36.191 --> 00:09:40.041
I mean, after all, how many people
really are disabled?

00:09:40.716 --> 00:09:42.956
I'm in the United States,

00:09:42.981 --> 00:09:46.605
so some of the figures and facts
that I will present this evening

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:48.782
are based on my area.

00:09:48.807 --> 00:09:52.384
But since this is global, I also
am going to show some figures

00:09:52.409 --> 00:09:55.469
and I'll make comments on some
other countries as well.

00:09:55.494 --> 00:09:59.020
Because accessibility is international.

00:09:59.843 --> 00:10:05.095
In the United States, 25.6 percent
of adults in the United States

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:07.289
have some type of disability.

00:10:07.314 --> 00:10:08.882
And that number fluctuates,

00:10:08.906 --> 00:10:12.851
because there are disabilities
that individuals are born with,

00:10:12.876 --> 00:10:19.120
there are disabilities that people suddenly
get because of an accident or an illness,

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:20.945
or surgery.

00:10:20.969 --> 00:10:23.623
There's disabilities that come with age.

00:10:24.659 --> 00:10:30.274
So that number is not just 25.6 percent.

00:10:31.668 --> 00:10:36.540
It's really a fluctuating number that
could affect every single one of us.

00:10:38.464 --> 00:10:43.403
Around the world, there
are more than one billion people

00:10:43.428 --> 00:10:45.520
living with some form of disability.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:48.610
So when someone says, is there
really a need for web accessibility,

00:10:48.635 --> 00:10:50.668
is there really people that need it?

00:10:50.693 --> 00:10:52.640
I kind of think there is.

00:10:52.665 --> 00:10:57.040
I kind of think that it's important
that we take a look at what

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:01.120
I know to be an invisible
segment of our society.

00:11:01.638 --> 00:11:05.015
When I ask people why
their site is not accessible

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.573
or why they don't consider accessibility,

00:11:08.273 --> 00:11:10.400
they don't think they have
ever seen or know anybody,

00:11:10.425 --> 00:11:12.930
but maybe that guy that just
passed him in the hallway,

00:11:12.954 --> 00:11:14.327
that might have a disability.

00:11:15.032 --> 00:11:16.999
They don't consider…

00:11:17.716 --> 00:11:19.600
how many people they're overlooking.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:24.773
It's an invisible segment of our society
that we need to bring visible.

00:11:25.169 --> 00:11:27.136
And it's not them versus us.

00:11:27.161 --> 00:11:29.866
It's not one group versus another.

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:31.331
It's all of us,

00:11:31.359 --> 00:11:32.937
it's all of us collectively…

00:11:34.179 --> 00:11:35.492
that make up this group.

00:11:36.453 --> 00:11:39.657
People with disabilities
are in a community

00:11:40.948 --> 00:11:42.799
that makes up a whole lot more

00:11:43.252 --> 00:11:44.815
than just the single individual,

00:11:45.272 --> 00:11:49.936
because it's their families,
their communities, their friends,

00:11:51.205 --> 00:11:54.752
the people that they do business with,
the people that they interact with,

00:11:54.777 --> 00:11:57.299
 it's a very powerful consumer base.

00:11:57.324 --> 00:11:59.363
And if a company has a website

00:11:59.388 --> 00:12:01.100
and they're not accessible,

00:12:01.129 --> 00:12:04.286
they're missing out on
this very powerful base.

00:12:05.296 --> 00:12:06.824
So then the next question comes up.

00:12:06.853 --> 00:12:08.553
Is there really a problem?

00:12:08.578 --> 00:12:12.586
I mean after all, how big a
problem, if there is one, how big is it?

00:12:13.119 --> 00:12:15.029
So for those of you that are familiar,

00:12:15.497 --> 00:12:20.274
there's a company WebAIM,
and every year they do an audit

00:12:20.299 --> 00:12:24.093
on the home page only,
of one million sites.

00:12:24.638 --> 00:12:29.611
And for 2019, actually I correct myself,
February of 2020

00:12:30.323 --> 00:12:33.333
they did their million sites evaluation

00:12:33.801 --> 00:12:38.592
and what showed up was, 98.1%

00:12:38.617 --> 00:12:41.500
of all the sites they tested had errors,

00:12:42.361 --> 00:12:46.295
had barriers to people with disabilities.

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:51.367
Ninety-eight point one percent of
the sites they tested out of one million,

00:12:53.775 --> 00:12:55.992
 less than 2% were fully accessible.

00:12:56.948 --> 00:12:59.439
And I've put on the screen
that big number because

00:12:59.465 --> 00:13:02.693
I want people to see that number.
I want it to be important.

00:13:02.718 --> 00:13:05.081
I want to let it be known

00:13:05.106 --> 00:13:07.908
that there is an opportunity
for us as web designers

00:13:07.933 --> 00:13:09.056
to make a difference.

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:11.414
It's a huge opportunity

00:13:12.142 --> 00:13:15.013
and the source link to that
is at the bottom of the screen.

00:13:18.129 --> 00:13:21.264
But are there other problems
with the lack of web accessibility?

00:13:21.288 --> 00:13:25.272
I mean other than just
WebAIM's One Million report?

00:13:25.952 --> 00:13:27.041
Absolutely.

00:13:27.512 --> 00:13:30.054
Because of the lack of web accessibility,

00:13:30.078 --> 00:13:35.083
we are seeing a drastic increase
in web accessibility lawsuits.

00:13:35.473 --> 00:13:39.981
As Joe mentioned, I work with clients
who are referred to me by attorneys.

00:13:40.464 --> 00:13:45.497
Since COVID started,
my phone, my email,

00:13:45.522 --> 00:13:48.737
people contacting me because they've
received demand letters,

00:13:48.762 --> 00:13:53.692
attorneys contacting me inquiring
about what can be done for their client.

00:13:53.716 --> 00:13:54.847
It has tripled.

00:13:55.776 --> 00:13:57.683
I can't handle all of these people.

00:13:58.678 --> 00:14:01.700
But so far we're doing
a good job at either

00:14:01.725 --> 00:14:03.672
working with them or referring them out,

00:14:03.697 --> 00:14:06.202
but that is a huge increase just for me.

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:09.931
So this is not going to go away.

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:13.354
Whether it's COVID where people are now,

00:14:13.378 --> 00:14:16.403
everyone is in their home
and relying upon the internet,

00:14:16.428 --> 00:14:19.110
or whether it's going to be after Covid,

00:14:19.135 --> 00:14:21.293
when it becomes real obvious

00:14:21.318 --> 00:14:25.542
that everyone deserves
the right to equal access

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:28.372
everywhere, including the web.

00:14:29.979 --> 00:14:34.919
And, personally, I appreciate
that there's attention being brought

00:14:34.944 --> 00:14:36.798
to the lack of web accessibility,

00:14:37.074 --> 00:14:39.379
I just don't care for these trolls at all.

00:14:40.224 --> 00:14:43.145
So the more that we make
websites accessible,

00:14:44.109 --> 00:14:45.763
the more these trolls…

00:14:45.788 --> 00:14:47.113
is going to make their…

00:14:48.344 --> 00:14:49.795
efforts more difficult.

00:14:51.634 --> 00:14:52.917
And that's a goal of mine.

00:14:54.787 --> 00:15:00.521
Here's an interesting piece of information
from the report from WebAIM.

00:15:01.401 --> 00:15:08.138
The top six common types of WCAG 2
failures on these sites that they tested,

00:15:08.191 --> 00:15:09.305
Look at this:

00:15:09.329 --> 00:15:15.516
Low contrast text, 86.3 percent of pages

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:18.835
had low contrast text.

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:23.171
Earlier today there was a
presentation about colors and contrast.

00:15:23.196 --> 00:15:26.392
So, hopefully, you attended
that particular presentation.

00:15:26.417 --> 00:15:29.971
If not, when these videos are available,

00:15:29.996 --> 00:15:35.535
I do hope that you go back and you watch
and listen to what Colleen had to say.

00:15:36.232 --> 00:15:38.328
Right at the top, Low Contrast.

00:15:39.517 --> 00:15:44.851
Personally, I don't understand why people
put gray font on a light gray background.

00:15:44.876 --> 00:15:46.241
That's never made sense to me,

00:15:46.266 --> 00:15:48.609
but I see that on so many sites,

00:15:49.694 --> 00:15:53.179
and obviously WebAIM
found something similar.

00:15:53.204 --> 00:15:57.319
The other one, Missing
alternative text for images, 66 %.

00:15:57.344 --> 00:16:01.862
Come on, that's like the one
thing we talk about the most.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:07.028
Empty links, Missing form
fields, Empty buttons,

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:09.637
Missing the document language?

00:16:09.662 --> 00:16:13.398
We're not even going to tell people
what the language of the website is?

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:20.022
How easy if you had a website
that had these six errors,

00:16:20.047 --> 00:16:24.227
they came to you, how easy would
it be for you to remediate that site?

00:16:25.664 --> 00:16:28.102
Keeping that in mind, why aren't they?

00:16:29.169 --> 00:16:30.479
That's my question.

00:16:30.503 --> 00:16:33.782
Maybe they need you to knock
on their door and tell them.

00:16:34.746 --> 00:16:39.800
Now, at the same time, I read
legal cases on a daily basis.

00:16:39.825 --> 00:16:42.352
I always tell people if
you can't go to sleep,

00:16:42.377 --> 00:16:46.232
something that's better than counting sheep
is to read some of these legal briefs.

00:16:47.359 --> 00:16:50.404
They're not really entertaining,
but they are educational

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:54.138
and the most common problems
referred in these cases,

00:16:54.163 --> 00:16:58.003
the website prevents screen readers
from accessing primary content.

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:03.600
Oh boy, here we go again,
no text description for an image,

00:17:04.624 --> 00:17:05.893
forms not working,

00:17:06.449 --> 00:17:11.124
you can't increase the text to 200 %
without loss of content or functionality?

00:17:12.179 --> 00:17:14.725
Here's one that a lot of people
don't think about.

00:17:14.749 --> 00:17:19.097
If you have something on your
website that has a time limit on it,

00:17:19.122 --> 00:17:22.774
you need to let people
have a way to adjust it,

00:17:22.799 --> 00:17:25.089
disable it, slow it down, restart it,

00:17:25.714 --> 00:17:28.201
instead of just timing them
out and kicking them off.

00:17:29.226 --> 00:17:32.232
Because a sighted person
would see if there's a countdown,

00:17:33.146 --> 00:17:35.487
but perhaps someone
with a disability either

00:17:36.145 --> 00:17:37.869
doesn't get the warning,

00:17:37.894 --> 00:17:39.853
can't read the warning,

00:17:39.878 --> 00:17:42.447
can't respond fast enough to the warning,

00:17:42.472 --> 00:17:44.426
because you didn't give them enough time.

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:48.447
Web pages without titles,
oh my goodness.

00:17:48.472 --> 00:17:50.819
It is so easy to put a
title in a WordPress site.

00:17:50.843 --> 00:17:52.184
How could… how?…

00:17:52.209 --> 00:17:53.461
I'm beside myself.

00:17:54.181 --> 00:17:58.062
The purpose of each link cannot be
determined from the text link alone,

00:17:58.087 --> 00:18:01.294
and that's where we get
a whole lot of those, "click here"

00:18:02.951 --> 00:18:07.944
And I believe we had someone just
a couple of presentations previous,

00:18:07.969 --> 00:18:09.634
that talked about just that.

00:18:10.252 --> 00:18:11.436
Very, very important.

00:18:12.769 --> 00:18:15.958
So you see that the problems
that are being referenced in lawsuits

00:18:15.983 --> 00:18:17.964
and the ones that WebAIM found

00:18:17.988 --> 00:18:20.601
are simple to remediate.

00:18:20.933 --> 00:18:23.139
They're simple to put in the first place,

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:28.714
but when you have a client come to
you with a site that has these problems,

00:18:28.739 --> 00:18:30.739
these are the
simple ones to fix.

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:34.889
So what's the solution
to the lack of accessibility?

00:18:34.914 --> 00:18:38.300
Very simple, make products
that are accessible to everyone

00:18:38.807 --> 00:18:39.948
out of the gate.

00:18:39.972 --> 00:18:44.454
We do know that now that there are
themes within the WordPress repository

00:18:44.479 --> 00:18:45.881
that are accessibility-ready.

00:18:46.538 --> 00:18:48.132
I do know there are

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:51.779
people that, that's all they will work with

00:18:51.803 --> 00:18:53.624
is an accessibility-ready theme.

00:18:53.649 --> 00:18:56.005
And then they make sure
that the content and the images

00:18:56.029 --> 00:18:58.341
and everything they add to it
remains accessible.

00:18:58.747 --> 00:19:00.000
 It makes their job easy.

00:19:01.093 --> 00:19:04.003
Remediate products and areas
that have barriers,

00:19:05.423 --> 00:19:07.644
support companies and products
that are accessible

00:19:07.669 --> 00:19:09.253
and innovate with new products.

00:19:09.627 --> 00:19:13.203
You may not have known,
that the disabled market

00:19:13.227 --> 00:19:15.379
or excuse me, the disabled community,

00:19:15.403 --> 00:19:17.655
is actually considered

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:20.391
to be extreme users of certain products.

00:19:20.678 --> 00:19:21.916
What that means is,

00:19:22.638 --> 00:19:24.345
what you may not also be aware of,

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:26.443
is that voice recognition software

00:19:26.468 --> 00:19:31.104
has been available for people
with disabilities for 20 plus years,

00:19:31.824 --> 00:19:38.011
and because people with disabilities
were using voice recognition technology,

00:19:39.752 --> 00:19:46.403
that same technology is now used
in Alexa and Siri and Google's assistant.

00:19:46.971 --> 00:19:50.465
So something that was done for a—

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:53.795
what was a select group of people
by their first analysis,

00:19:53.820 --> 00:19:57.489
is something that has now
been rolled out for everyone.

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:01.275
So it's not a small group.

00:20:01.299 --> 00:20:04.845
It's all of us that benefit
from these products.

00:20:07.149 --> 00:20:09.929
But let's move in to what people
want to know about tonight

00:20:09.954 --> 00:20:13.958
and that is, the Increased
Value of Accessibility

00:20:13.983 --> 00:20:16.000
for both Designers and Companies.

00:20:16.921 --> 00:20:20.380
What you hear a lot is,
accessibility can drive innovation,

00:20:20.405 --> 00:20:22.063
such as what I just mentioned,

00:20:22.087 --> 00:20:24.623
it can enhance your brand or hurt it.

00:20:24.648 --> 00:20:25.996
It helps SEO,

00:20:26.731 --> 00:20:28.518
it can minimize legal risk

00:20:29.017 --> 00:20:31.309
and then it can extend your market reach.

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:37.607
And yet, those five points to some
people are just vague.

00:20:38.529 --> 00:20:41.946
Yeah, maybe, but what about me?

00:20:42.837 --> 00:20:45.356
What can it do for me, for my company?

00:20:45.826 --> 00:20:47.735
or what can it do for me?

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:50.312
I'm a designer
with a small agency,

00:20:50.336 --> 00:20:51.347
What can it do?

00:20:52.131 --> 00:20:53.979
So look, I want to remind you,

00:20:56.594 --> 00:20:58.580
if my slides will progress,

00:21:00.112 --> 00:21:05.683
this market, 98.1% of
the market needs you,

00:21:07.306 --> 00:21:09.536
for your web accessibility knowledge,

00:21:09.888 --> 00:21:11.119
expertise,

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:14.879
to go in there and make a difference,

00:21:15.549 --> 00:21:17.568
98%.

00:21:18.143 --> 00:21:22.098
So when I hear people tell me,
well you know what, I… [sighs]

00:21:22.123 --> 00:21:24.106
my client doesn't want to pay
for accessibility.

00:21:24.975 --> 00:21:26.067
I don't know what to do?

00:21:26.834 --> 00:21:29.688
Ninety-eight percent…

00:21:29.712 --> 00:21:32.865
of the marketplace… needs you.

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:40.153
So if that client doesn't want
to be inclusive

00:21:40.177 --> 00:21:42.071
and bring accessibility in

00:21:42.095 --> 00:21:45.226
and you have done your
best to educate them,

00:21:46.639 --> 00:21:50.260
maybe you need to turn your attention
to somebody who will appreciate you

00:21:50.284 --> 00:21:53.776
and your skills, and your
commitment and your dedication.

00:21:54.748 --> 00:21:56.125
Just saying.

00:21:58.476 --> 00:22:00.695
But then we come back to,
well what can I charge?

00:22:01.301 --> 00:22:04.137
You know I mean, I don't know,
how can I mark this up?

00:22:04.643 --> 00:22:06.288
How do I make a living doing this?

00:22:07.545 --> 00:22:13.405
When I first had a website made for me,
it was in 1996, 97-ish,

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:16.944
the person charged me $3, 000.

00:22:18.025 --> 00:22:20.890
I didn't think anything of it
at the time, other than, phew,

00:22:20.915 --> 00:22:22.199
that's a lot of money,

00:22:22.224 --> 00:22:24.130
but I also thought that
must be the going rate,

00:22:24.155 --> 00:22:26.255
because I had nothing
else to compare it to.

00:22:27.104 --> 00:22:30.175
But the person that designed my website
was not very pleased,

00:22:30.204 --> 00:22:32.137
because I kept asking questions

00:22:32.162 --> 00:22:34.956
and I wanted this little
tweak and just that change,

00:22:34.981 --> 00:22:38.057
and can we do this, and let's move that

00:22:38.082 --> 00:22:40.152
and he made a statement one day
and said, look

00:22:40.699 --> 00:22:43.969
I just don't have time for all your
questions. If you want answers,

00:22:43.993 --> 00:22:45.309
go take a class

00:22:46.162 --> 00:22:47.562
and get the answers for yourself.

00:22:49.458 --> 00:22:52.694
So I did and I never had to
ask him another question,

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:55.344
but I also never had to
pay him any more money,

00:22:55.369 --> 00:22:57.881
because I knew how then to do it myself.

00:22:58.579 --> 00:23:00.893
So trust me, I never say that to clients,

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:07.384
but I look back at that and when I
first started working websites myself,

00:23:07.409 --> 00:23:10.224
I knew I couldn't charge $3,000.

00:23:10.249 --> 00:23:17.289
I didn't have that much intelligence,
expertise, information, experience,

00:23:17.314 --> 00:23:19.436
even though I'd just taken a class.

00:23:20.038 --> 00:23:22.083
I didn't think I could and I didn't.

00:23:22.936 --> 00:23:25.196
So I came up with a value calculator

00:23:25.221 --> 00:23:27.981
and here's where if you have
a pen or pencil handy

00:23:28.006 --> 00:23:31.679
or you take notes on a notepad or
something, I'd like you to consider this.

00:23:32.464 --> 00:23:34.370
When you think about what is your value

00:23:34.394 --> 00:23:38.045
when you include accessibility
into your services,

00:23:39.855 --> 00:23:41.974
what do you charge for a basic website

00:23:42.874 --> 00:23:44.800
prior to adding any accessibility to it?

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:46.986
Put down that figure.

00:23:48.255 --> 00:23:51.689
Now I want you to
consider your personal value.

00:23:52.375 --> 00:23:54.830
If you have taken classes
on accessibility,

00:23:55.382 --> 00:23:56.569
put a plus.

00:23:57.107 --> 00:23:59.428
If you have certificates or degrees,

00:23:59.792 --> 00:24:00.864
put a plus.

00:24:01.756 --> 00:24:04.587
If you have any business
awards or accolades,

00:24:04.612 --> 00:24:08.896
whether they're related to accessibility
or just because you have integrity

00:24:08.921 --> 00:24:12.643
and you are a good business person
and you take care of your clients,

00:24:12.668 --> 00:24:13.805
give yourself a plus.

00:24:14.751 --> 00:24:16.798
If you are a disabled person yourself

00:24:16.822 --> 00:24:20.051
or someone in your family
or a friend that you see often,

00:24:20.552 --> 00:24:21.596
put a plus.

00:24:22.236 --> 00:24:25.245
If you have employees
that are disabled that work for you,

00:24:25.884 --> 00:24:26.912
put a plus.

00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:29.713
If you have an understanding of WCAG,

00:24:30.159 --> 00:24:32.361
for each level of understanding,
put a plus,

00:24:32.831 --> 00:24:33.963
or a big plus.

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:42.201
If you are very involved in a particular
niche market that you know real well,

00:24:43.062 --> 00:24:46.215
you know what the market is,

00:24:46.240 --> 00:24:50.361
what drives them to do what
they do in their business, put a plus.

00:24:50.386 --> 00:24:52.228
My niche market is attorneys.

00:24:52.253 --> 00:24:55.150
It's been attorneys for
the last 10 and a half years.

00:24:55.175 --> 00:24:58.332
So I know attorneys.
I know the legal marketplace,

00:24:58.357 --> 00:25:00.721
I know what they're allowed to do
and what they're not allowed to do

00:25:00.746 --> 00:25:02.762
when it comes to marketing
and advertising.

00:25:02.787 --> 00:25:04.651
I am always networking with them.

00:25:04.971 --> 00:25:06.177
So I know that market.

00:25:06.201 --> 00:25:08.711
If you have a market like that,
give yourself a plus.

00:25:08.736 --> 00:25:10.377
Give yourself a plus for every year

00:25:10.401 --> 00:25:13.157
that you already have
been including accessibility.

00:25:13.858 --> 00:25:15.211
If you can write code,

00:25:15.744 --> 00:25:17.709
give yourself a couple of big pluses.

00:25:18.655 --> 00:25:23.057
And if you often network with
other accessibility professionals,

00:25:23.082 --> 00:25:24.337
 give yourself a plus.

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:29.735
And then think about what else
sets you apart from everyone else.

00:25:30.505 --> 00:25:31.942
That is a huge plus.

00:25:33.931 --> 00:25:37.255
When you look at all those pluses you're
going to put a dollar amount to that,

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:38.500
not with me tonight,

00:25:38.525 --> 00:25:40.276
but at a time when
you're going to sit down,

00:25:40.301 --> 00:25:42.956
because this is something
that is personal to you.

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:45.223
Not what everybody else is charging,

00:25:45.248 --> 00:25:46.857
but what is your value.

00:25:47.197 --> 00:25:48.702
When you understand your value

00:25:48.731 --> 00:25:50.769
you can explain it to your clients

00:25:50.794 --> 00:25:52.296
and that's the important part.

00:25:53.013 --> 00:25:54.811
So you start with that value,

00:25:55.584 --> 00:25:59.777
then you go to something
where it requires manual efforts.

00:25:59.802 --> 00:26:05.611
This is an excerpt, a clipping from one
of the checklists that we use in my office.

00:26:05.636 --> 00:26:08.721
And it's a spreadsheet
that has on the left column

00:26:08.746 --> 00:26:13.054
whatever action that we need to take
in order to remediate a site,

00:26:13.079 --> 00:26:14.678
 who's going to do that?

00:26:14.703 --> 00:26:17.414
is that person someone that
works internal in my company

00:26:17.414 --> 00:26:20.285
or do we have to outsource,
and what is the cost?

00:26:20.754 --> 00:26:24.635
Every person that works on a project,
if they're internal,

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:27.384
they have either a salary
or a negotiated amount

00:26:27.408 --> 00:26:28.664
that they are paid.

00:26:28.689 --> 00:26:30.339
If we outsource it,

00:26:30.364 --> 00:26:32.900
we are paying whatever
they tell us is their value

00:26:32.925 --> 00:26:34.260
to do that for us.

00:26:35.339 --> 00:26:36.414
We go down the list and we

00:26:36.438 --> 00:26:39.436
make sure that we cover all our bases.

00:26:39.461 --> 00:26:46.338
We do test everything on JAWS and NVDA
by a native user of that.

00:26:47.295 --> 00:26:50.133
When we are remediating images,
we don't just…

00:26:50.822 --> 00:26:53.312
throw in the alt description
and call it a day.

00:26:53.337 --> 00:26:56.028
We research and maybe we
have to find them a new image

00:26:56.053 --> 00:26:59.140
if the one that is on their site
is really not that great.

00:26:59.746 --> 00:27:02.028
Maybe it needs editing,
because it's too pixelated,

00:27:02.053 --> 00:27:04.694
maybe it's too big,
maybe it doesn't fit the content?

00:27:05.216 --> 00:27:08.688
We're going to change the meta info
and the geo info for search

00:27:09.151 --> 00:27:10.507
and we're going to do all that,

00:27:10.905 --> 00:27:14.279
because we go the extra effort.

00:27:14.304 --> 00:27:19.100
What we're doing for our client is more
than just fixing the technical problems,

00:27:19.125 --> 00:27:22.184
we want a good user experience
for them as our client

00:27:22.209 --> 00:27:25.453
and their website for their users
and their visitors.

00:27:25.478 --> 00:27:29.234
So think about this type of an effort

00:27:29.259 --> 00:27:33.904
and what value would
you put into each one of those?

00:27:33.929 --> 00:27:36.945
How would you separate yourself
from everyone else?

00:27:37.852 --> 00:27:40.782
A lot of times people
calculate an hourly rate.

00:27:40.807 --> 00:27:42.565
I talked to someone this week

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:45.483
who told me—he came to me
and asked me if I'd hire him,

00:27:45.508 --> 00:27:48.185
because he couldn't afford
to be freelance anymore.

00:27:48.185 --> 00:27:51.597
So I looked at what he charged a client
to work on a website

00:27:51.622 --> 00:27:54.712
And how many extra hours got
thrown in that he didn't calculate.

00:27:55.268 --> 00:27:58.112
The end of the day, he was
making less than $7 an hour.

00:27:59.026 --> 00:28:01.432
So in Southern California,
you can't live on that.

00:28:02.788 --> 00:28:07.965
So I told him, if you're going to
calculate your value by an hourly rate,

00:28:08.331 --> 00:28:09.344
ask yourself,

00:28:09.836 --> 00:28:13.258
what is your resentment number.

00:28:14.092 --> 00:28:20.514
What is the lowest amount you would ever
allow a client to say:

00:28:21.047 --> 00:28:23.246
"This is how much I'll pay you per hour"

00:28:24.019 --> 00:28:26.350
and take that number
and multiply it by four.

00:28:27.177 --> 00:28:30.080
You should never work at a level

00:28:31.017 --> 00:28:34.062
that causes you to not enjoy
the work that you're doing,

00:28:34.087 --> 00:28:37.450
because accessibility
can be a whole lot of fun.

00:28:37.475 --> 00:28:39.200
The people that you will meet,

00:28:39.225 --> 00:28:40.887
the clients when they get excited,

00:28:40.911 --> 00:28:42.209
it's just fun.

00:28:42.864 --> 00:28:48.298
And then you have more value,
updates and maintenance plans.

00:28:48.323 --> 00:28:50.911
In a lot of the settlements we're seeing
in these legal cases,

00:28:50.936 --> 00:28:52.262
they're asking the court

00:28:52.826 --> 00:28:56.710
to require that people
test their sites every six months.

00:28:57.506 --> 00:28:59.036
That's a maintenance plan for you.

00:28:59.733 --> 00:29:02.377
So that's how you add value,

00:29:02.402 --> 00:29:05.070
that's how you add more to you.

00:29:05.095 --> 00:29:09.524
Your total value then becomes
your unique value, your cost per hour,

00:29:09.549 --> 00:29:11.150
your team that you work with,

00:29:11.175 --> 00:29:14.016
the complexity of the site that
you're going to be working on,

00:29:14.041 --> 00:29:18.213
and how well your skills
and expertise can do that,

00:29:18.723 --> 00:29:21.893
the timeline—
rush jobs always cost more

00:29:22.488 --> 00:29:23.825
and future offerings,

00:29:25.402 --> 00:29:28.685
your updates and your maintenance plans.

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:33.619
Then you put that into a strategy.

00:29:34.666 --> 00:29:36.960
When I first started
in business in the 80s,

00:29:36.984 --> 00:29:38.067
long time ago,

00:29:38.559 --> 00:29:42.523
I was out in my neighborhood
asking businesses,

00:29:42.548 --> 00:29:45.864
why were they successful
and the guy next door wasn't?

00:29:45.889 --> 00:29:47.935
Why did they continually succeed

00:29:47.935 --> 00:29:50.855
when people across the street
kept going out of business?

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:54.815
And what I heard more times
from the successful business,

00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:56.329
is they had a plan.

00:29:56.354 --> 00:29:57.780
They didn't wing it.

00:29:57.809 --> 00:30:01.934
They put in a plan and they planned it out
as far as they could.

00:30:02.521 --> 00:30:03.748
They put in that plan,

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:07.457
not only the type of customers
and clients they wanted,

00:30:07.482 --> 00:30:10.111
they put in that plan the type
of education they may need

00:30:10.135 --> 00:30:13.344
in order to satisfy those clients,

00:30:13.368 --> 00:30:14.814
they put in that plan

00:30:15.120 --> 00:30:19.442
what would happen if
business really picked up?

00:30:20.238 --> 00:30:25.078
So many people plan in the minute
and worry about something going wrong,

00:30:25.718 --> 00:30:27.680
but as I said a couple seconds ago,

00:30:27.705 --> 00:30:30.220
my phone calls and my emails have…

00:30:31.367 --> 00:30:33.486
really exploded in the last few months.

00:30:34.111 --> 00:30:36.857
Fortunately, I have a list of people
that I can call upon,

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:40.783
that I'm able to make sure
that those contacts

00:30:40.808 --> 00:30:42.483
and those inquiries were taken care of.

00:30:43.120 --> 00:30:46.773
Take the information about
your value, your expertise,

00:30:46.798 --> 00:30:49.691
what kind of websites you want to work
on, and what you want to do,

00:30:49.716 --> 00:30:51.036
and put it into a plan

00:30:52.432 --> 00:30:53.842
and follow that plan.

00:30:55.314 --> 00:30:58.236
Be transparent with your client
but don't be invisible.

00:30:59.497 --> 00:31:03.365
What we do for our clients,
is we either work in Basecamp or Asana.

00:31:03.390 --> 00:31:05.100
 We work in Slack and Toggl

00:31:05.683 --> 00:31:08.864
and we give our clients
reports all the time.

00:31:09.442 --> 00:31:11.259
They know exactly
what we're doing for them,

00:31:11.284 --> 00:31:12.596
so they don't have to worry.

00:31:13.033 --> 00:31:14.115
And what we do,

00:31:14.454 --> 00:31:17.050
is we audit their website
and we give them a report.

00:31:17.633 --> 00:31:19.371
And we may audit that website

00:31:19.917 --> 00:31:21.413
with an automatic tester,

00:31:21.889 --> 00:31:24.500
before we put our hands on it
and manually test it,

00:31:24.539 --> 00:31:26.042
and we will give them that report,

00:31:26.409 --> 00:31:29.165
but then we make sure that they see

00:31:29.699 --> 00:31:32.673
the difference it makes with
someone who is keyboard only,

00:31:33.146 --> 00:31:35.154
and someone who is screen reader.

00:31:36.199 --> 00:31:39.271
That way they get a better
understanding of their target market.

00:31:39.295 --> 00:31:42.824
They get a better personal
understanding of accessibility.

00:31:43.891 --> 00:31:47.815
I have been working with
accessibility and websites for 12 years,

00:31:47.840 --> 00:31:49.226
maybe a little bit longer.

00:31:49.250 --> 00:31:51.912
And I was brought to my knees humbly,

00:31:52.424 --> 00:31:53.442
at that point.

00:31:53.466 --> 00:31:56.927
I had been designing websites
and I thought I was doing a great job.

00:31:56.952 --> 00:31:59.954
And I thought I was really
proud of what I had,

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:04.248
and then a member of
my family, who is blind,

00:32:04.974 --> 00:32:08.733
had the benefit of someone coming
to show her how to use a screen reader

00:32:08.758 --> 00:32:11.025
to access the web and visit websites.

00:32:12.157 --> 00:32:15.641
I was emotionally blown away
by how much freedom it gave her,

00:32:16.441 --> 00:32:21.449
but I also was humbled when I gave the
man my URLs of the sites I was so proud of

00:32:21.474 --> 00:32:22.983
and asked him to test them.

00:32:23.007 --> 00:32:24.641
And not a single one of them…

00:32:25.323 --> 00:32:27.840
could be read by a screen reader.

00:32:29.596 --> 00:32:31.144
My world changed that day.

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:35.152
And so I'm hoping that as you
get accustomed to accessibility,

00:32:35.177 --> 00:32:36.818
your world takes a shift

00:32:36.843 --> 00:32:40.051
and you shift to where this just
becomes part of what you do,

00:32:40.076 --> 00:32:42.129
don't even have to think about it.

00:32:42.154 --> 00:32:44.815
So after you get the test and
you find out what you're doing

00:32:45.440 --> 00:32:47.249
and you start working with your client,

00:32:47.274 --> 00:32:49.451
please do the easy stuff first.

00:32:49.476 --> 00:32:50.949
and the reason for this is,

00:32:50.973 --> 00:32:53.872
if you are working on the
most difficult part of the website

00:32:53.897 --> 00:32:56.016
where you possibly have to write
some new code

00:32:56.041 --> 00:32:58.167
and redesign certain things,

00:32:58.192 --> 00:33:00.157
your client doesn't see what you're doing.

00:33:00.182 --> 00:33:04.800
He doesn't see that you're making
any sort of progress at all,

00:33:04.825 --> 00:33:08.797
but if you go through the little things
first or what I call the easy things,

00:33:08.797 --> 00:33:10.986
not little, they're all important,

00:33:11.011 --> 00:33:16.270
the easiest things first and then
show your client as you go along.

00:33:16.295 --> 00:33:17.776
Your client sees progress.

00:33:17.801 --> 00:33:19.279
Your client sees hope

00:33:19.304 --> 00:33:22.190
and knows that you're doing
what you'd promised to do.

00:33:23.885 --> 00:33:27.159
We always follow the WCAG, the checklist.

00:33:27.184 --> 00:33:29.721
I have this spreadsheet on the screen

00:33:29.746 --> 00:33:33.144
that shows the guidelines and
where they fall under WCAG.

00:33:33.170 --> 00:33:35.722
In all of our spreadsheets we reference

00:33:35.746 --> 00:33:39.632
not only with internally to
each other, but to our clients,

00:33:39.657 --> 00:33:44.620
which web content accessibility guidelines
success criteria we are meeting for them.

00:33:45.485 --> 00:33:47.022
They don't care,

00:33:47.047 --> 00:33:48.441
but they do care
that we tell them.

00:33:49.017 --> 00:33:52.046
They don't understand it, but
they care that we tell them

00:33:52.071 --> 00:33:53.927
and that's the transparency of it.

00:33:53.951 --> 00:33:55.827
And that's what's important.

00:33:55.861 --> 00:33:59.220
Plus when you follow the WCAG,
you learn the WCAG.

00:33:59.245 --> 00:34:02.666
When you do this, the simple stuff first

00:34:03.600 --> 00:34:05.937
you learn. It's easier to learn.

00:34:07.183 --> 00:34:10.476
So basically you can have an
increased profit with your business

00:34:10.971 --> 00:34:13.277
by increasing your value to your client,

00:34:14.049 --> 00:34:16.634
increasing the fees that
you're going to charge them,

00:34:16.659 --> 00:34:19.613
because of the value of what
you're bringing to the table.

00:34:19.638 --> 00:34:21.504
This will increase your business income

00:34:22.482 --> 00:34:24.582
and unless you go out
and buy a brand new Ferrari,

00:34:24.607 --> 00:34:26.716
it should be an increase in your profit.

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:31.114
But I want you to notice that
one thing I didn't say,

00:34:31.139 --> 00:34:34.058
I didn't talk about plug-ins
as part of this process,

00:34:34.083 --> 00:34:36.535
because it's good, bad
and ugly with WordPress.

00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:38.883
The good is there's plugins
that can do work for you.

00:34:40.175 --> 00:34:43.002
The bad is, a lot of those plugins
will make the site worse

00:34:43.027 --> 00:34:45.348
and you're now going to have
to figure out how to remediate

00:34:45.372 --> 00:34:47.267
over the plugin that
you just installed.

00:34:47.780 --> 00:34:51.216
And the ugly is that there are plugins
that through their marketing efforts

00:34:51.240 --> 00:34:54.384
are going to try to tell you
that they'll do your job for you.

00:34:55.078 --> 00:34:57.946
 And then when they don't,

00:34:58.598 --> 00:35:00.392
you're the one that's in trouble.

00:35:01.527 --> 00:35:02.998
So don't be the bad apple.

00:35:03.909 --> 00:35:05.456
Be willing to put in the effort.

00:35:05.958 --> 00:35:08.289
You will enjoy it,
you'll get excited, trust me.

00:35:10.133 --> 00:35:12.807
Now we'll get to the second half
and I promise

00:35:14.278 --> 00:35:18.167
that this is a good part of it that
you'll be able to share with your clients.

00:35:18.617 --> 00:35:20.778
The Profitability for Companies.

00:35:20.803 --> 00:35:23.674
People with disabilities
are not a solitary market.

00:35:23.699 --> 00:35:26.550
They are surrounded by family
members and friends.

00:35:27.122 --> 00:35:30.710
They recognize products and services
that accommodate people in society.

00:35:31.236 --> 00:35:35.252
They will refer to people

00:35:36.952 --> 00:35:38.590
when it is an accessible website.

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:42.963
So you want to show
them the money.

00:35:43.524 --> 00:35:49.752
In the U.S., the total after-tax
disposable income for working age people

00:35:49.776 --> 00:35:55.033
with a disability is 490 billion dollars.

00:35:56.002 --> 00:35:57.435
That's a lot of money.

00:35:57.460 --> 00:36:00.283
Discretionary income which is
the income that they get to spend

00:36:00.308 --> 00:36:04.876
after they've paid all their debts
and their taxes is still 21 billion.

00:36:05.954 --> 00:36:07.940
Most people aren't aware of these numbers.

00:36:07.965 --> 00:36:10.293
You want to share these with your clients.

00:36:11.760 --> 00:36:13.640
Interesting note that we discovered,

00:36:13.665 --> 00:36:15.558
is that individuals
with hearing difficulties

00:36:15.583 --> 00:36:19.672
represent the disability category with the
greatest amount of discretionary income,

00:36:20.636 --> 00:36:23.226
followed by people
with vision disabilities.

00:36:23.250 --> 00:36:27.699
So when you heard about captions
from Meryl earlier today

00:36:28.244 --> 00:36:29.832
this shows why that's important.

00:36:29.856 --> 00:36:31.744
You can explain that to your clients.

00:36:32.234 --> 00:36:33.697
If you're in the UK,

00:36:34.495 --> 00:36:40.409
what we have there is,
internet users are 10.7 million

00:36:40.433 --> 00:36:41.772
or 10.07.

00:36:41.809 --> 00:36:44.430
Sixty-nine percent
of people will leave a site

00:36:45.260 --> 00:36:49.851
if they have a problem with access
and they will go someplace else.

00:36:49.876 --> 00:36:51.864
This is called the Click-Away Pound report

00:36:51.890 --> 00:36:56.450
and in my resources I do have a link
that you can read this entire report

00:36:56.474 --> 00:36:58.903
and share it with your clients.

00:37:00.191 --> 00:37:02.032
I also have a Nielsen report

00:37:02.069 --> 00:37:05.611
that gives all the information
for US companies,

00:37:06.175 --> 00:37:08.985
but the worst case scenario
that you can tell your clients

00:37:09.793 --> 00:37:12.232
is that the cost of a
lawsuit is far greater.

00:37:12.918 --> 00:37:17.095
An attorney retainer
can be up to $5,000,

00:37:17.120 --> 00:37:19.108
the settlement costs, the loss of work.

00:37:20.030 --> 00:37:21.578
Those are real figures

00:37:21.602 --> 00:37:25.467
and I do not lead with this
with my clients.

00:37:25.492 --> 00:37:29.428
I lead with the numbers for
the market that they can reach.

00:37:29.453 --> 00:37:30.811
If they don't want to listen,

00:37:30.835 --> 00:37:32.523
then I'll bring this part in.

00:37:33.245 --> 00:37:35.965
But how should they market
to the disabled community?

00:37:36.773 --> 00:37:42.190
Besides developing products that
meet the needs of people with disabilities.

00:37:42.215 --> 00:37:44.683
They should get involved in the community.

00:37:44.708 --> 00:37:46.994
They also need to employ
people with disabilities.

00:37:47.430 --> 00:37:48.767
Inclusive advertising,

00:37:49.375 --> 00:37:51.963
put an accessibility statement
on their website,

00:37:51.988 --> 00:37:53.975
because far too
many designers think,

00:37:54.000 --> 00:37:57.498
well I'll just go in and tell my client
if they make their website accessible,

00:37:57.523 --> 00:37:59.013
that people will flock to it.

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:00.700
That's not true.

00:38:00.945 --> 00:38:04.273
It will give a boost to the SEO
in some cases,

00:38:04.298 --> 00:38:06.765
but they still need to do their marketing.

00:38:06.790 --> 00:38:10.810
It's essential and this is the type
of marketing that they need to do.

00:38:11.918 --> 00:38:14.910
If they do this marketing,
if they do it genuine,

00:38:15.630 --> 00:38:18.232
they will see a return
of that marketplace,

00:38:18.257 --> 00:38:20.373
because like any other community,

00:38:20.398 --> 00:38:23.901
people with disabilities do talk
about people that treat them right.

00:38:24.983 --> 00:38:27.083
And when you think about
your price and your value

00:38:27.108 --> 00:38:31.272
just remember there's always somebody
who's willing to do it cheaper.

00:38:31.297 --> 00:38:33.712
So don't cut yourself short

00:38:33.737 --> 00:38:36.536
when you're talking
to your clients about your value.

00:38:36.536 --> 00:38:39.154
Make sure that you go for the big value.

00:38:40.140 --> 00:38:43.265
So some of the references
or resources that I'll share with you,

00:38:43.290 --> 00:38:46.320
is the automatic accessibility
checkers that we use.

00:38:46.708 --> 00:38:49.997
We don't use one more than another

00:38:50.025 --> 00:38:51.641
except, the top two.

00:38:51.665 --> 00:38:53.470
We love the Deque axe Pro beta,

00:38:53.494 --> 00:38:56.448
because of the type of reports
that we can give our client

00:38:56.473 --> 00:38:59.295
and we love the Tenon API

00:38:59.320 --> 00:39:02.430
which you can put in your website,

00:39:02.455 --> 00:39:04.588
so that you can see as you're working,

00:39:05.268 --> 00:39:07.683
what you're doing and
what needs to be done.

00:39:08.293 --> 00:39:11.379
So you don't have to wait till
something is done to go, did I do it right?

00:39:11.403 --> 00:39:15.657
By putting the API on your website for this
one, you'll be able to watch your progress

00:39:15.683 --> 00:39:17.939
 and learn as you go along.

00:39:18.746 --> 00:39:22.990
Other resources that I mentioned, these
are places where you can learn knowledge:

00:39:23.657 --> 00:39:27.012
The Web Accessibility Initiative at w3.org

00:39:27.037 --> 00:39:31.277
has a free course on the
introduction to web accessibility,

00:39:31.302 --> 00:39:34.477
and you can get a certificate
for completing that course

00:39:34.502 --> 00:39:36.673
and it is a great course.

00:39:36.697 --> 00:39:37.748
I've done that course.

00:39:37.795 --> 00:39:40.714
I refer people to that course.
It's full of knowledge.

00:39:40.739 --> 00:39:44.441
Knowbility, excellent resource,
Deque, WebAIM, CSUN,

00:39:44.466 --> 00:39:47.262
University of Illinois,
University of Washington,

00:39:47.287 --> 00:39:49.397
the information is available.

00:39:49.422 --> 00:39:51.626
The educational resources are there.

00:39:52.317 --> 00:39:55.044
If you need to have a native user to test,

00:39:55.068 --> 00:39:58.057
here are three places where you can have,

00:39:58.081 --> 00:40:01.677
you can contract with them
for someone or gain a referral.

00:40:01.702 --> 00:40:06.182
Again, Knowbility AccessWorks,
Pearson for the Blind and CSUN

00:40:06.207 --> 00:40:08.080
which is here in Southern California.

00:40:10.129 --> 00:40:13.841
Also in my slides will be the
links and resources to the statistics

00:40:13.866 --> 00:40:15.459
that I quoted for the companies,

00:40:15.483 --> 00:40:19.021
for the number of people in the
community and the spending power.

00:40:20.133 --> 00:40:24.063
So, in summary, 98.1% of websites
need remediation.

00:40:24.250 --> 00:40:26.948
One in four people
have a recognized disability.

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:30.768
Accessibility is international.

00:40:30.793 --> 00:40:34.396
It's a right, it's a personal right,
it's a civil right.

00:40:34.421 --> 00:40:37.023
Plan your design to include accessibility,

00:40:37.048 --> 00:40:39.491
have a strategy for remediation,

00:40:39.515 --> 00:40:43.028
understand the why and
how of your value to a client,

00:40:43.702 --> 00:40:47.436
understand the potential market
so you can explain it to your client.

00:40:48.106 --> 00:40:50.535
Check the obvious and fix those first—

00:40:50.559 --> 00:40:53.353
fonts, structure, color
schemes, video, audio.

00:40:53.963 --> 00:40:56.611
Don't rely only on automatic testers.

00:40:56.636 --> 00:40:57.911
Test your site manually.

00:40:58.574 --> 00:41:01.801
Have a native user of accessibility
technology test your site.

00:41:02.426 --> 00:41:06.146
Be transparent with your clients
and support the community.

00:41:06.682 --> 00:41:08.520
And I know I talked fast.

00:41:08.549 --> 00:41:11.265
I wanted to give you a lot of information

00:41:11.290 --> 00:41:14.342
and since I know we're being recorded,
you can come back

00:41:16.179 --> 00:41:18.607
and visit this information again.

00:41:18.632 --> 00:41:20.540
But I do believe if there are questions,

00:41:20.564 --> 00:41:23.070
maybe I have a couple minutes
to answer questions.

00:41:23.767 --> 00:41:25.776
<v Joe> Yes you do, thank you so much Sumner.

00:41:25.800 --> 00:41:28.129
What a great presentation, thank you.

00:41:29.713 --> 00:41:31.152
All right, let me refresh here.

00:41:31.176 --> 00:41:34.246
Our first question  is:

00:41:34.271 --> 00:41:36.698
Is there a study answering
the following question:

00:41:36.723 --> 00:41:41.226
How many people leave a web page
because of accessibility failure?

00:41:41.251 --> 00:41:44.092
<v Sumner> Sixty-seven percent.
It's in the Click-Away Pound report.

00:41:44.763 --> 00:41:47.164
<v Joe> Okay
<v Sumner> And the report also indicates

00:41:47.189 --> 00:41:49.469
that when people leave that website,
they don't give up,

00:41:49.835 --> 00:41:52.114
they do spend that money elsewhere.

00:41:52.669 --> 00:41:53.763
So it's a total loss.

00:41:54.236 --> 00:41:56.904
<v Joe> All right, do you feel designer and
developer reluctance

00:41:56.928 --> 00:41:58.807
 in folding in accessibility

00:41:58.832 --> 00:42:02.777
is that they undercharge
or overestimate their bottom line?

00:42:02.802 --> 00:42:06.982
How would you recommend they address
this or can you share a brief experience?

00:42:07.931 --> 00:42:11.165
<v Sumner> My recommendation would be that they

00:42:13.329 --> 00:42:15.405
go through that list
of what I did for value

00:42:15.984 --> 00:42:16.984
and accept…

00:42:17.870 --> 00:42:21.148
more about what their
personal value is as a designer.

00:42:21.173 --> 00:42:24.562
And if they've
undervalued themselves,

00:42:24.587 --> 00:42:26.960
this is probably a personal issue

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:28.550
that they need to take and look at.

00:42:28.575 --> 00:42:30.571
And they might want to
sit and work with someone.

00:42:30.596 --> 00:42:33.570
I sit with designers all the
time that want to discuss,

00:42:33.595 --> 00:42:37.643
how can I increase what I charge?
I don't know what my assets are?

00:42:37.667 --> 00:42:41.076
So if a person will take a list of
what are their personal assets,

00:42:41.102 --> 00:42:42.721
like the list I rang off

00:42:42.746 --> 00:42:44.451
and then reevaluate.

00:42:44.476 --> 00:42:48.100
What does happen, is many times they
don't charge enough, they get burned out.

00:42:48.125 --> 00:42:49.910
And then it becomes resentment

00:42:49.934 --> 00:42:51.184
and that doesn't work,

00:42:52.211 --> 00:42:54.207
but at the same time
whatever amount they charge

00:42:54.232 --> 00:42:57.742
you don't want to overcharge because you're
going to have to live up to that amount.

00:42:58.532 --> 00:43:00.227
<v Joe> All right, next question,

00:43:01.573 --> 00:43:04.729
do you think that web
development is driven too much by

00:43:04.754 --> 00:43:07.327
developers needing
to rapidly solve problems

00:43:07.966 --> 00:43:10.819
defined by business rather than by users?

00:43:15.500 --> 00:43:18.611
<v Sumner> Not sure if I understand that question.

00:43:18.636 --> 00:43:20.463
<v Joe> Okay, let me, let me restate it.

00:43:20.487 --> 00:43:23.285
Do you think that web development
is driven too much

00:43:23.310 --> 00:43:27.403
by developers who are trying to solve
business related problems

00:43:27.428 --> 00:43:31.680
instead of trying to solve the problem
in terms of how a user would use it?

00:43:32.556 --> 00:43:34.545
<v Sumner> So if I understand that correctly,

00:43:34.570 --> 00:43:37.268
a client's going to come to
a designer and say

00:43:37.293 --> 00:43:39.791
I need a website built,
because I need to sell a product,

00:43:41.025 --> 00:43:43.520
instead of I need a website
built that's the best

00:43:43.520 --> 00:43:45.917
for user experience so
I can get more business.

00:43:45.942 --> 00:43:46.965
<v Joe> Correct

00:43:46.990 --> 00:43:49.535
<v Sumner> Okay, well yeah, clients don't know.

00:43:49.559 --> 00:43:50.933
We have to educate them.

00:43:50.958 --> 00:43:52.700
That's what they need from us.

00:43:52.724 --> 00:43:55.317
They need us to say,
"hey, you know what, that's great"

00:43:55.359 --> 00:43:59.493
let me help you understand how we
can make that dream of yours even bigger

00:43:59.517 --> 00:44:00.700
and more profitable.

00:44:01.146 --> 00:44:04.190
So we as designers need
to also be educators.

00:44:04.215 --> 00:44:06.940
Once we understand it, we can explain it.

00:44:09.440 --> 00:44:12.273
<v Joe> All right, this is a really brief one,

00:44:12.298 --> 00:44:15.588
For the non-legal,
what is a demand letter?

00:44:15.613 --> 00:44:17.493
You mentioned that in your presentation.

00:44:17.517 --> 00:44:19.476
What does that mean in
relation to accessibility?

00:44:20.372 --> 00:44:23.540
<v Sumner> Demand letter is… [laughs]

00:44:23.565 --> 00:44:25.756
it's actually a form letter unfortunately,

00:44:25.781 --> 00:44:27.635
but it comes in on an attorney letterhead

00:44:27.660 --> 00:44:29.923
and it says dear website owner

00:44:31.645 --> 00:44:36.057
I'm sending you this letter
because my client, a blind user,

00:44:36.082 --> 00:44:39.053
or a person with another disability,

00:44:39.078 --> 00:44:40.811
attempted to visit your website

00:44:40.836 --> 00:44:45.313
and there was barriers that
prevented this person from equal access.

00:44:46.048 --> 00:44:48.786
So it's important that you
contact our office to discuss

00:44:48.811 --> 00:44:52.706
some form of remuneration
for the difficulties

00:44:52.731 --> 00:44:56.441
and the problems that
my client has endured.

00:44:57.085 --> 00:45:00.173
And that is the precursor
to them filing a case.

00:45:00.198 --> 00:45:01.592
They send out the demand letter,

00:45:01.617 --> 00:45:04.468
because they want someone to call and go
"please, please please"

00:45:04.516 --> 00:45:06.240
and beg that you don't hurt them.

00:45:06.810 --> 00:45:11.026
When most of those demand
letters don't turn into cases,

00:45:11.051 --> 00:45:12.434
but many of them do.

00:45:12.458 --> 00:45:13.524
In either case,

00:45:14.118 --> 00:45:16.750
it's important to consult with
an attorney how to respond.

00:45:17.828 --> 00:45:20.340
<v Joe> Awesome. Okay our final question is,

00:45:20.752 --> 00:45:21.899
[coughs]
excuse me,

00:45:21.924 --> 00:45:27.047
if you build a website
with accessibility in mind

00:45:27.071 --> 00:45:28.830
and the same site without it

00:45:29.664 --> 00:45:33.674
what do you think the difference in cost
would be between the two sites

00:45:33.698 --> 00:45:35.100
in terms of what you can charge?

00:45:36.639 --> 00:45:37.824
<v Sumner> Uh, hmm

00:45:38.171 --> 00:45:42.106
<v Joe> That's a difficult question.
I'm not sure...

00:45:42.135 --> 00:45:44.704
<v Sumner> Well, I don't know that I can answer it,

00:45:44.729 --> 00:45:47.160
because if I go back to
when I was building sites

00:45:47.184 --> 00:45:48.950
before I knew anything about accessibility

00:45:48.975 --> 00:45:50.511
and I know what I charge then,

00:45:51.112 --> 00:45:52.700
versus what I charge now,

00:45:53.800 --> 00:45:59.470
I don't know how I would back out
all my accessibility efforts

00:45:59.494 --> 00:46:00.766
to try to figure out

00:46:02.110 --> 00:46:03.222
what that would be.

00:46:05.489 --> 00:46:06.537
<v Joe> I'm just asking—

00:46:06.800 --> 00:46:09.096
<v Joe> Yeah, I was going to
say I wouldn't, I mean

00:46:09.120 --> 00:46:10.811
<v Sumner> I can't even figure that out.

00:46:10.836 --> 00:46:12.914
<v Joe> Yeah, that's a difficult question.
[Sumner laughing]

00:46:12.938 --> 00:46:16.710
It reminds me, I used
to speak at high schools

00:46:16.736 --> 00:46:19.864
or go to the school when they
had the parents come to work

00:46:19.889 --> 00:46:23.081
and there would always be a kid who
would say, how much do you make?

00:46:23.106 --> 00:46:24.151
which is…

00:46:24.176 --> 00:46:26.663
I mean it's a random question.
I'm not making fun of it, but

00:46:26.688 --> 00:46:28.504
it's a difficult question to quantify,

00:46:28.529 --> 00:46:32.112
because your value is based
on that formula you talked about.

00:46:32.137 --> 00:46:36.299
<v Sumner> Exactly and too many times I see
in forum posts and discussion groups

00:46:36.324 --> 00:46:40.344
where someone will say, well okay I'm going
to build a five-page website for a client,

00:46:40.368 --> 00:46:41.654
what would you charge?

00:46:41.679 --> 00:46:43.563
Well first off that's
not enough information

00:46:43.588 --> 00:46:45.435
to find out what that price would be,

00:46:45.460 --> 00:46:48.460
but on the other hand don't
choose somebody else's number.

00:46:49.645 --> 00:46:51.855
Your value and their value
are totally different

00:46:52.193 --> 00:46:59.596
and I really believe that when we get
into that thinking pattern where we say,

00:46:59.621 --> 00:47:01.462
what is it I'm bringing to the table here?

00:47:02.247 --> 00:47:04.598
And be real clear of what
I'm bringing to the table

00:47:04.623 --> 00:47:06.605
has a big value to this client,

00:47:06.630 --> 00:47:10.608
because no one else is
bringing this same value.

00:47:11.362 --> 00:47:13.847
And I'm going to make
a difference to this client

00:47:13.872 --> 00:47:16.779
and their website and the user experience

00:47:16.805 --> 00:47:19.169
that no one else is going to
do, because they're not me.

00:47:20.556 --> 00:47:22.878
And it's not ego, it's confidence.

00:47:23.952 --> 00:47:25.901
<v Joe> Great, well thanks again Sumner,

00:47:25.926 --> 00:47:27.763
what are you up to in the next few weeks?

00:47:27.787 --> 00:47:31.104
or what can we look forward
to hearing from you with?

00:47:31.129 --> 00:47:33.975
<v Sumner> Well, I've got a lot of speaking
engagements, it seems,

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:36.698
you know since we're not traveling
the country or the world right now.

00:47:36.723 --> 00:47:38.178
Everybody's on Zoom.

00:47:38.203 --> 00:47:42.698
So I have a lot of that, and
we're planning for a next meetup

00:47:42.723 --> 00:47:45.215
that's coming up that we're
going to do another hands-on.

00:47:45.240 --> 00:47:49.215
We have the WordCamp Los Angeles
that's coming up in a couple of weeks.

00:47:49.240 --> 00:47:51.602
So there's a lot still going on.

00:47:52.642 --> 00:47:57.289
<v Joe> Awesome, well thanks again for sharing
with this incredible international audience

00:47:57.314 --> 00:47:58.900
and I know we appreciate it.

00:47:58.925 --> 00:48:00.690
<v Sumner> For people that want to—for,
I didn't—sorry,

00:48:00.690 --> 00:48:02.156
I didn't mean to interrupt, Joe,

00:48:02.181 --> 00:48:06.439
but for people that want to follow
me I'm Sumner Davenport on Twitter,

00:48:06.464 --> 00:48:08.641
I'm Sumner Davenport on LinkedIn

00:48:08.666 --> 00:48:13.300
or please go to the meetup and look
for the WordPress San Fernando Valley.

00:48:13.326 --> 00:48:16.298
And since we're on Zoom, you can
come in from anywhere in the globe

00:48:16.322 --> 00:48:20.498
and participate and contribute
and let's all learn together.

00:48:21.237 --> 00:48:24.620
<v Joe> How can I be so rude
and not let you lean on that.

00:48:24.644 --> 00:48:25.969
[Sumner laughing]

00:48:26.015 --> 00:48:27.486
All right thank you again, Sumner.

00:48:27.511 --> 00:48:28.539
<v Sumner> Thank you, Joe.

00:48:28.564 --> 00:48:32.194
<v Joe> All right I personally like to thank
